course: dictionary
lesson: 11428
title: Angry words and bitter feelings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how strong a feeling is. Acrimonious is a formal word for an argument where people are full of anger and bitterness. «The meeting was acrimonious: people shouted, insulted each other and stormed out.» The clues are shouted, insulted and stormed out, so the meeting was angry and bitter, not just a little bit cross.
explain: Acrimonious is not the same as a small disagreement. «The club had a friendly debate about where to go on the trip» is not acrimonious, because nobody was angry. Ask yourself: were there harsh words and bitter feelings? If yes, acrimonious fits.
explain: You will mostly meet this word in news, stories and serious writing, often about arguments, meetings, or a divorce or split that went badly. Check the meaning by putting «full of angry, bitter feelings» into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found it.
fact: Acrimonious means full of angry, bitter feelings and harsh words.
fact: «An acrimonious debate» is one where the people argue with real anger, not just a polite difference of opinion.
fact: «An acrimonious split» means two people or groups part after a bitter fight.
fact: Acrimonious is a formal word, so it is used more in writing and news than in everyday talk.
fact: A mild or friendly disagreement is not acrimonious, because acrimonious needs real anger and bitterness.
exercise: «The meeting was acrimonious, with people shouting and insulting each other.» What does acrimonious mean? | Calm and friendly | Very short | *Full of angry, bitter feelings | Funny and light-hearted
exercise: Which situation is most likely to be described as acrimonious? | A class picnic where everyone shares the cake | A quiet reading session in the library | *Two neighbours who yell insults over the fence and refuse to speak again | A walk in the park with the dog
exercise: «The divorce was long, painful and acrimonious.» Which word means full of angry, bitter feelings? | painful | long | divorce | *acrimonious
test: «After the vote, the councillors had an acrimonious meeting, and nobody would shake hands.» What does acrimonious suggest about the meeting? | Quiet and polite | *Angry, bitter and full of harsh words | Happy and joyful | Short and fast
test: Which sentence describes an acrimonious debate? | *Each side called the other liars and the debate ended in shouting | The class voted on a name for the new pet rabbit | The speakers agreed on almost everything | Everyone listened politely and thanked each other at the end
exam: Jayden says, «The talks were acrimonious, so everyone must have been happy.» What is wrong with his idea? | *Acrimonious means full of angry, bitter feelings, not happiness | Acrimonious means very quick | Acrimonious means happy and calm | Acrimonious means nothing at all
